+/*
+ * ===========================================================================================
+ * Root classification for condemned collectible contexts (stats kinds 11-24)
+ * ===========================================================================================
+ *
+ * WHY. A downstream WASM host doing repeated collectible-ALC load/unload cycles observes the
+ * teardown reporting NotQuiescent forever: some root keeps the LoaderAllocator witnesses
+ * alive, and an exhaustive managed-side root trace found nothing — which leaves the roots
+ * managed reflection CANNOT enumerate (raw GCHandles, thread stacks/registers, native
+ * structures). These kinds narrow that down without a debugger or a heap dump.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS MEASURED — and what it does NOT prove. For every live GC handle of the FOUR SCANNED
+ * TYPES (normal, pinned, weak, weak-track-resurrection — sgen's fifth handle table holds
+ * weak-FIELD registrations rather than user handles and is not walked at all), the target object
+ * is attributed to the condemned set (collectible + unloading contexts) in one of two ways:
+ *
+ *   CLASS attribution (kinds 11-14, bucketed by handle type): the TARGET's class belongs to
+ *   one of the condemned memory managers.
+ *
+ *   IDENTITY attribution (kind 19, class-blind but NOT table-blind — it too sees only handles
+ *   of those same four scanned types): the TARGET *is* one of the condemned
+ *   contexts' own runtime objects — the managed AssemblyLoadContext wrapper (which
+ *   PrepareForAssemblyLoadContextRelease deliberately promotes to a STRONG handle while an
+ *   unload is in flight) or a manager's LoaderAllocator witness. Those objects are
+ *   corelib-classed, so class attribution is blind to them by construction; counting them
+ *   separately keeps the runtime's own expected roots distinguishable from third-party ones.
+ *
+ * Attribution is PER HANDLE and NON-TRANSITIVE. A non-zero bucket therefore establishes
+ * "a GC handle DIRECTLY TARGETS an object attributable to the condemned set" — a NECESSARY,
+ * not sufficient, condition for "a GC handle is the holder". All-zero buckets do NOT establish
+ * that no GC handle is involved: rooting is transitive, and a handle whose immediate target is
+ * corelib-classed while its object graph reaches a condemned instance lands in NO bucket. The
+ * miss classes, enumerated so nobody has to rediscover them:
+ *
+ *   - containers: object[] / List<object> / Dictionary<,> / boxes holding app instances;
+ *   - the reflection-cache family: RuntimeType, RuntimeAssembly, MethodInfo/FieldInfo — all
+ *     corelib-classed even when they DESCRIBE collectible metadata (this is where a real
+ *     residual root was already found downstream);
+ *   - delegates: Action/Func are corelib types; the target instance and method are not;
+ *   - pinned byte[]/T[] buffers held for interop (element class corelib);
+ *   - a strong handle placed directly on a LoaderAllocator that kind 16 counts as live, unless
+ *     it happens to be one of the identity objects kind 19 covers;
+ *   - objects registered in sgen's HANDLE_WEAK_FIELDS table (sgen_register_obj_with_weak_fields):
+ *     that fifth table is outside the scan entirely, so such a registration is invisible to
+ *     every kind here — including kind 22, which sums only the four scanned tables;
+ *   - anything reached only through native structures or thread stacks/registers.
+ *
+ * The sibling lifecycle test ForcedUnload_RootedPointerFreeObjects_BlockNativeFree already
+ * demonstrates that BCL-typed roots block the free, i.e. exactly the shape this classification
+ * cannot see. Read these kinds as "which handle classes DIRECTLY hold the set, and how many
+ * witnesses are still live" — a triage funnel, never a proof of absence.
+ *
+ * ATOMICITY, and why the latch alone is not enough. Every gauge is a field of ONE snapshot:
+ * kind 17 recomputes the whole snapshot (set build + one bounded handle walk + witness count)
+ * and returns its GENERATION TOKEN; the gauge kinds then read that latched result. Independent
+ * per-kind recomputation would be mutually inconsistent — a context reclaimed between two reads
+ * yields "every bucket 0 while witnesses are live", manufacturing precisely the false signal
+ * this exists to rule out.
+ *
+ * But a single process-global latch does NOT by itself make a multi-read atomic, because the
+ * gauge reads carry no generation of their own. Two LOGICAL callers are enough to break it, with
+ * no native thread race at all: caller A takes kind 17 and reads kind 11, yields; caller B takes
+ * kind 17; A resumes and reads kind 12 — out of B's generation. A now holds a tuple that never
+ * existed. The protocol therefore makes the read TOKEN-BOUND:
+ *
+ *   1. kind 17 — refresh, and remember the generation token it returns;
+ *   2. read the gauges;
+ *   3. kind 20 — read the latched token WITHOUT refreshing. If it is NOT EQUAL to step 1's,
+ *      somebody else refreshed mid-read: DISCARD the tuple and retry from step 1.
+ *
+ * Kind 20 exists solely to be that token: it is the one kind that never recomputes anything, so
+ * a reader can always ask "is what I just read still one generation?" without perturbing it.
+ * The regression CondemnedSnapshot_MixedGenerationRead_IsRejectedByTheSequenceToken drives the
+ * A/B interleaving deterministically in one thread and requires the mixed tuple to be rejected.
+ *
+ * THE TOKEN IS A NON-ZERO WRAPPING GENERATION, NOT A MONOTONIC CLOCK — and the difference is
+ * load-bearing, so it is stated here rather than left implicit. The counter runs 1, 2, 3, ...,
+ * G_MAXINT32, 1, 2, ... It carries exactly one guarantee: consecutive snapshots have DIFFERENT
+ * tokens. It carries NO ordering guarantee, because at the G_MAXINT32 -> 1 transition the newer
+ * generation's token is numerically SMALLER than the older one's.
+ *
+ *   * Consumers MUST compare tokens with != / ==, NEVER with < / >. A freshness test written as
+ *     "token > the one I remembered" silently rejects every read taken across the wrap; an
+ *     ordering test written that way reports the newest generation as stale.
+ *   * The alternative — saturating at G_MAXINT32 so the token only ever grows — was tried and
+ *     is WRONG: a frozen token makes kind 20 report "still one generation" for every subsequent
+ *     mixed read, so the surface stops detecting tears permanently. Wrapping costs at most one
+ *     undetected tear per 2^31 snapshots; saturating costs all of them, forever.
+ *   * Kind 24 (test-only) seeds the counter just below G_MAXINT32 so the wrap, and the
+ *     interleaving across it, are reachable from a regression rather than only by argument.
+ *
+ * COST, and the bound that actually holds. The snapshot is O(managers + budget): membership is a
+ * pointer-keyed set, so it does not degrade quadratically as contexts strand — the state this
+ * diagnostic explains — and it short-circuits entirely on an empty condemned set. The handle
+ * walk is bounded on RAW SLOTS, not on classified hits, which is the only bound worth stating:
+ * each sgen handle table is an SgenArrayList whose `next_slot` is a persistent high-water mark
